---
import GuideLayout from '@layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import ReferenceBlock from '@components/ReferenceBlock.astro';
import { config } from './config';
import { toolPath, categoryPath } from '@lib/paths';
import { guidePathBySlug, toolPathBySlug } from '@lib/tools/tool-link';
import { categories } from '@data/categories';
import type { EcosystemEntry } from '@data/types';

interface Props {
  title: string;
  description: string;
  readMinutes: number;
  updatedAt: string;
  entry: EcosystemEntry;
}

const props = Astro.props;
const category = categories.find(c => c.slug === config.categorySlug)!;
const toolHref = toolPath({ slug: config.slug, segment: category.segment });
const normalizeHref = toolPathBySlug('normalize-whitespace');
---

<GuideLayout
  {...props}
  toolSlug={config.slug}
  toolName={config.name}
  toolCategorySlug={config.categorySlug}
>
  <section id="quick-answer">
    <h2>Quick Answer <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Remove blank lines to delete empty lines and remove extra lines from any text. This line cleanup
      will remove line breaks that are purely whitespace, pulling the remaining content together to
      compact text while keeping the order. To strip blank lines from a document, paste and convert
      in one click.
    </p>
    <a href={toolHref} class="cta-link">Try Remove Blank Lines →</a>
  </section>

  <section id="why-it-matters">
    <h2>When Blank Lines Get In The Way <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Empty lines accumulate when you paste between apps, export data with trailing rows, or write
      with generous spacing. They are harmless in prose but become a problem when you need a clean
      list, want to import data that rejects blank rows, or are pasting into a field with a tight
      length limit.
    </p>
    <ReferenceBlock type="key-idea" heading="Content lines are never touched">
      <p>
        Only blank lines are removed. Every line with visible text keeps its place and its line
        break, so you never lose real content.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>

  <section id="how-it-works">
    <h2>How It Works <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      The tool splits your text into lines, drops any line that is empty or contains only spaces
      and tabs, and rejoins the rest. In other words, it will delete blank lines throughout your text.
      This is how to compact code or data without gaps: for example, a list with blank rows between
      every item becomes a compact, contiguous list with no gaps.
    </p>
    <p>
      This tool is free and runs online entirely in your browser. Your text stays on your device, protecting your privacy: nothing is uploaded to a server, and results appear instantly.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="examples">
    <h2>Examples <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      <strong>A double-spaced list.</strong> For example, a list with a blank row between every item
      collapses to one clean line per item, ready to paste into a field that rejects empty rows.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>An export with trailing gaps.</strong> For example, a data dump ending in several
      empty rows comes back with those rows gone and the real content intact.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="common-mistakes">
    <h2>Common Mistakes <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Removing the blank lines that separate paragraphs">
      <p>
        This tool removes gaps, not paragraph structure on purpose, but that cuts both ways. In
        Markdown and HTML, blank lines between paragraphs are meaningful: they tell the
        renderer where one paragraph ends and another begins. If you remove all blank lines from
        Markdown, your paragraphs will merge into a single block. Use this tool on plain lists and
        data, not on Markdown or HTML source.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Whitespace-only lines survive if you expect them to be removed">
      <p>
        A line that contains a single space or tab looks blank but is not empty. This tool removes
        those too: any line whose only content is spaces or tabs is treated as blank and deleted.
        If you want to keep those lines, use a different approach.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>

  <section id="which-cleanup">
    <h2>Which Cleanup Do You Need? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>You want</th><th>Use</th><th>Line breaks</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Drop empty and whitespace-only lines</td><td>Remove Blank Lines</td><td>Kept between lines with text</td></tr>
        <tr><td>One line with single spaces</td><td>Normalize Whitespace</td><td>All removed</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section id="normalize-whitespace">
    <h2>Normalize Whitespace To One Line <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Whitespace normalization is the more aggressive cleanup, and it handles tabs and breaks, not
      just spaces. It is broader than a simple double-space fix, so use it to fix messy spacing and
      restore whitespace consistency in one step. It will collapse tabs and spaces, carriage returns and newlines: each run becomes
      one space, and the ends are trimmed. In other words, it will clean whitespace and standardize
      spacing in one pass, which also makes it the way to standardize whitespace across content
      pasted from many sources. The result is single line text with consistent spacing, which is what a CSV cell,
      a search box, a single-line database column or a URL parameter expects.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Before and after.</strong> An address pasted as "12  Park Road", then "  Pune", then an
      empty line normalizes to "12 Park Road Pune". A non-breaking space (U+00A0) copied from a web
      page counts as whitespace too, so it collapses like any other space. Text that is only spaces
      becomes empty, so check for an empty result before saving to a required field.
    </p>
    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Normalizing text whose lines matter">
      <p>
        Because it removes every line break, separate paragraphs and list items merge. When the line
        structure must survive, remove blank lines here instead, or use Remove Extra Spaces to fix
        doubled spaces inside each line.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
    <a href={normalizeHref} class="cta-link">Try Normalize Whitespace →</a>
  </section>

  <section id="order-of-operations">
    <h2>Order Of Operations <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Trim first, then remove blank lines, so lines that held only stray spaces are already empty.
      Normalize last, and only when one line is the goal, because it undoes every line break the
      other steps kept. Windows files end lines with a carriage return, which counts as whitespace in
      both tools, so a blank Windows line is removed and normalization folds the return into a space.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="remove-blank-lines-vs-alternatives">
    <h2>Remove Blank Lines vs Other Cleanup Tools <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Remove Blank Lines targets empty rows only and never touches lines that have content.
      The closest alternatives are <strong>Remove Duplicate Lines</strong>, which removes
      repeated lines (including repeated blank lines) while keeping unique ones, and
      <strong>Trim Text</strong>, which strips leading and trailing whitespace from each line.
      Lines that hold only spaces are already removed here, so reach for Trim Text when lines keep
      their content but carry stray edges, and see how to <a href={guidePathBySlug('trim-text')}>trim whitespace</a> in Excel
      and code. <a href="#normalize-whitespace">Normalize Whitespace</a> is a more aggressive alternative that also
      removes all line breaks, merging the text into a single line. If joining every line is what
      you want, you are after <a href={guidePathBySlug('remove-line-breaks')}>removing line breaks instead</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="related-tools">
    <p>
      <strong>Related tools:</strong> explore all text case and cleanup utilities in the
      <a href={categoryPath(category)}>Text Utilities</a> collection.
    </p>
  </section>

</GuideLayout>

<style>
  .cta-link {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    height: var(--touch-target);
    padding-inline: var(--space-5);
    margin-top: var(--space-5);
    background: var(--color-accent);
    color: var(--color-text-inverse);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
    font-size: var(--text-base);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: var(--transition-color);
  }
  .cta-link:hover { opacity: 0.9; text-decoration: none; }
</style>
